  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Elevated tide cycles persist due to Post- \nTropical Cyclone Josephine well off the coast, resulting in \nwidespread minor coastal flooding continuing for back bay areas \nin Ocean County, NJ and Sussex County, DE.\n\nPost-Tropical Cyclone Josephine has pulled away from the region\nas it's now over the Canadian Maritimes. However elevated water\nlevels persist, mainly in the back bay areas of Ocean County, \nNJ and Sussex County, Delaware. As a result we expect another \nround of minor coastal flooding this evening with the high tide \ncycle. Along the oceanfront areas of these counties as well as \nour other counties along the Atlantic coast and Delware Bay, any\ntidal flooding this evening looks to be very spotty in nature. \nThe trend is for water levels to be gradually diminishing \nhowever it's possible coastal flooding for could even linger \nthrough Saturday for the back bays. The Coastal Flood Advisory \ncurrently in effect runs through midnight tonight but we will \nevaluate later this evening whether it needs to be extended. \n\nAt this time we are not forecasting any coastal flooding for \nthe Maryland eastern shore. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Frost and freeze impacts tonight and Saturday \nnight where the growing season continues. \n\nA Freeze Warning was issued for areas north and west of the \nI-95 corridor from midnight until 9 AM Saturday where the \ngrowing season remains active. For the I-95 corridor and areas \nsouth and east, a Frost Advisory was issued from midnight until \n9 AM Saturday for all other zones, excluding Sussex County in \nDelaware and the barrier islands in New Jersey.\n\nAs an upper-level trough pivots across the Northeast tonight, a\nstrong mid-level ridge in the Plains to Midwest shifts eastward\nthrough the weekend. This will support strong surface high \npressure shifting eastward into the Northeast and Mid-Atlantic \nthrough the weekend.\n\nA chilly and dry airmass will accompany this strong surface \nhigh, and as the high itself settles more overhead, forecast \nsoundings show surface winds decoupling. This will set the stage\nfor efficient radiational cooling both tonight and Saturday \nnight. The forecast lows are in the upper 20s to mid 30s for \nmuch of the area (with the only exception along the immediate \ncoastal strip) with a favorable setup for frost and freeze. Some\nscattered to broken cirrus will be in place overnight. However,\nthe thin cloud coverage will be the only limiting reagent to \nthe efficient radiational cooling. This will probably slow \ncooling down by a few degrees at best, likely aiding to prevent \na widespread freeze situation, but it is not expected to slam \nthe brakes hard on the efficient radiational cooling setup \novernight.\n\nAn additional round of frost/freeze impacts is anticipated \nSaturday night through Sunday morning for wherever the growing \nseason continues.",
